  "slug": "halloween",
  "title": "Halloween Color Palettes",
  "tagline": "Orange against ink black and dusky violet, from a 1933 colour dictionary that never heard of Halloween.",
  "description": "Halloween colour is orange and black, usually with a purple, and the usual problem is that both the orange and the purple arrive at full saturation and look like a party-store bag. These combinations come from Sanzo Wada's 1933 Dictionary of Color Combinations, plus one traditional Japanese pairing of red-lead orange and ink. Wada's plate 46 is simply orange and black; others set an orange against a dusky madder violet, a sepia or a deep indigo, which reads as autumn night rather than costume. Use them for invitations, posters, packaging, seasonal social graphics and table settings.",
